  "summary_md": "Furnishings companies are splitting into two starkly different narratives: traditional home-goods names are managing choppy housing-driven demand, while a cluster of micro-caps is pivoting entirely away from the sector. Margin discipline is the common thread—FLXS, LCUT, HBB, and HOFT are protecting profitability and capturing share despite soft revenues—while PRPL, LOVE, and BSET show tentative stabilization. The most distinctive stories sit at the extremes. ATER exits e-commerce for tantalum trading and AI-driven mineral exploration after a going-concern warning, sparking extreme speculative volume. SNBR juggles a full product reset with survival financing and an activist overhang. XWIN and MWYN are recasting themselves around AI infrastructure, with XWIN adding SpaceX-linked optionality. These pivots, alongside EFOI’s energy-storage shift, introduce acute execution risk as micro-caps ask investors to underwrite entirely new business models. NCL appears as an outlier, pairing strong cruise demand with a guidance cut and Elliott Management involvement.",
